<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atossa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 550–475 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Utauθa, Hutaosā, Atusa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atossa was an Achaemenid queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, wife first of her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius the Great</a>, and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a>. After Darius overthrew the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Gaumata</a>, he married her and her sister Artystone, and the union with the line of Cyrus reinforced his claim to the throne; Atossa became his primary consort. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> credits her with urging Darius to send an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and around 487 BCE she secured the succession of her son Xerxes, though he was not Darius's eldest son. Nothing is known of her death; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>' tragedy The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> shows her still alive when Xerxes invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queens such as <b>Atossa</b> and Parysatis had real influence, though no woman ever ruled.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pasargadae, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.2,53.18&amp;year=-502" aria-label="Pasargadae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5930 1061 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Atossa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6380 1331h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6380 1331h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.2,53.18&amp;year=-502">What was here in 502 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
